2026, day 84

Good morning from Asia.

‘US sent Iran 15-point plan aimed at ending the Middle East war’ (Reuters);

‘Trump says Iran gave the US gift ‘worth a tremendous amount of money’’ (SCMP);

‘Trump declares Iran war ‘won’ as Tehran denies claims of peace talk progress’ (WaPo);

‘Mediators Aim for U.S.-Iran Meeting by Thursday’ (WSJ) - “Officials from Turkey, Egypt and Pakistan are pushing to have talks arranged between U.S. and Iranian officials, but both sides are far apart”;

‘Iran has expressed a preference for negotiating with Vance, sources say’ (CNN);

‘Nuclear-armed Pakistan mediating between Iran, US seen as 'highly problematic': 'Saudis could influence'’ (Ynet);

‘Saudi Leader Is Said to Push Trump to Continue Iran War in Recent Calls’ (NYT) - “Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman sees a “historic opportunity” to remake the region, according to people briefed by U.S. officials on the conversations”;

‘Saudi official reiterates Kingdom’s rebuttal of claims it favors prolonging Iran conflict’ (Al Arabiya);

‘US to deploy more troops even as Donald Trump praises Iran peace talks’ (FT);

‘Drone attack hits fuel tank at Kuwait International Airport causing fire’ (Reuters);

‘Iran Moves to Assert Control Over Strait of Hormuz’ (WSJ);

‘Iran says ‘non-hostile’ ships can transit Strait of Hormuz’ (FT) - “Tehran tells IMO member nations that vessels must co-ordinate with it to pass through vital waterway”;

‘Trump’s first mistake was starting the war. His next mistake may be to let Iran win’ (Globe and Mail);

‘Israel Hits Russian-Iranian Weapons Smuggling Route in the Caspian Sea’ (WSJ) - “Moscow and Tehran have used the world’s largest inland sea to move everything from drones to oil and wheat”;

‘Smotrich says Litani River should be Israel’s new border with Lebanon’ (ToI);

‘Board of Peace envoy lays out principles of disarmament plan presented to Hamas’ (ToI);

‘European officials investigate possible Hezbollah links to antisemitic attack wave’ (Euractiv);

‘Tangled trail probably leads from Golders Green ambulance attack to Tehran’ (Guardian);

‘EU enlargement chief calls on countries to find a way for new members to join’ (Politico);

‘China is mapping the ocean floor as it prepares for submarine warfare with the U.S.’ (Reuters);

‘US unlikely to convince China to join new nuclear arms control agreement, experts say’ (SCMP);

‘G7 leaders seek summits with Takaichi as they try to balance China, US ties’ (Nikkei Asia) - “Japan seen as leading partner to foster cooperation through common values”;

In geoeconomics, ‘Donald Trump broke the WTO. Now he’s quietly re-engaging’ (Politico) - “Washington has launched a low-key campaign to tilt the rules of global trade back in its favor”;

‘Europe facing fuel shortage within days, warns Shell boss’ (Telegraph);

‘EU won’t reverse Russian gas ban or slow down green transition, says energy chief’ (Politico);

‘Russian oil import ban dropped from Brussels short-term agenda’ (Euractiv);

‘Philippines declares fuel emergency; South Koreans told to limit driving’ (AFR);

‘Hedge fund Millennium explores shifting Dubai staff to Jersey’ (FT);

‘VW in talks with Israel’s Iron Dome maker to shift from cars to missile defence’ (FT);

‘Anduril, Palantir Are Developing Golden Dome Missile Shield’s Software’ (WSJ;

‘US should give up on trying to change EU tech rules, lawmakers say’ (Politico);

‘Starmer to bring back 76 EU laws’ (Telegraph) - “New legislation will allow Labour to transfer Brussels powers back onto the UK statute book”;

In politics, ‘Danish election: Mette Frederiksen wins — but tricky coalition talks await’ (Politico);

‘Prosecutor admits government lacks evidence of misconduct by Fed chair’ (WaPo);

In markets, ‘Oil Drops on Reports of US Diplomatic Push to End War With Iran’ (BBG);

‘Bond Market Nears ‘Inflection Point’ After War-Related Shakeup’ (BBG)
